WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.010
You have a ring that makes you invisible; zero chance of being caught, no punishment, no one will ever find out.

2
00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:12.900
Is there still something you wouldn't do — and if so, whose voice exactly is the one that stops you?

3
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.510
Perhaps what we call conscience is nothing but a watchman planted inside us.

4
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.720
It is well past midnight.

5
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.930
Three in the morning.

6
00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:35.700
You are in the middle of the city, at a completely empty four-way intersection.

7
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.590
The light is red.

8
00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:44.600
You look right, you look left: not a car, not a pedestrian, not a traffic patrol waiting on the corner.

9
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.930
You are not even certain there is a camera at the junction.

10
00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.900
Your foot is on the brake, your hand rests on the gear stick.

11
00:00:51.900 --> 00:01:04.030
And for those two or three seconds, one of humanity's oldest questions is born again inside your car, without your opening a single book, without your hearing a single philosopher's name: if nobody is going to see,

12
00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.560
why am I stopping?

13
00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:13.890
This question was first set down on paper about two thousand four hundred years ago, in Plato's dialogue The Republic.

14
00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:20.420
A young man named Glaucon sits down opposite Socrates and tells him a deeply unsettling story.

15
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.790
There was a shepherd in Lydia.

16
00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:30.600
One day there was a great storm and an earthquake, the ground split open, and a deep chasm appeared in the place where he grazed his flock.

17
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.410
The shepherd gave in to his curiosity and climbed down.

18
00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:41.580
Below he found a bronze horse, a small door in the horse's side, and behind the door a corpse larger than a human body.

19
00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.430
On the finger of the corpse was a golden ring.

20
00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.560
He took the ring, climbed back out, and returned to his flock.

21
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:57.650
Some time later, when the shepherds gathered as they did every month to make their report to the king, the man was toying with the ring and turned its setting toward his palm.

22
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:02.260
In that moment the men beside him began to speak of him as though he were not there.

23
00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:05.910
Startled, he turned the setting back and became visible again.

24
00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:11.960
He tried it, and tried it again; setting inward, invisible; setting outward, visible.

25
00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:16.890
And the moment he understood this, what do you suppose was the first thing he did?

26
00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:21.180
He had himself chosen for the delegation going to the palace.

27
00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:27.710
He reached the palace, seduced the queen, killed the king together with her, and took the throne.

28
00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:32.480
Glaucon's real blow is not the story itself but the question that follows it.

29
00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:41.730
He says: if there were two such rings, and we gave one to the man we consider the most honest and the other to the man we consider the most corrupt, the outcome would not differ.

30
00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.260
Both would take the same road.

31
00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:49.670
Because nobody is solid enough to stay honest the moment they are certain of going unpunished.

32
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:57.480
On this view, the thing we call justice is not a beauty inside a person but the shadow left inside by a fear that comes from outside.

33
00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:02.570
Reduce the chance of getting caught to zero, and morality drops to zero as well.

34
00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:07.500
Now let us be honest: the ring Glaucon describes is no longer a fairy tale.

35
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.430
In the twenty-first century that ring is in everybody's pocket.

36
00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:14.960
Its name is the anonymous account.

37
00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:24.210
That empty box you can write in without showing your face, without giving your name, without paying any price, is the most widespread ring of invisibility in history.

38
00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.380
And we all know how people speak there.

39
00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:38.550
The same person, on the same day, on the same subject, can be courteous and measured somewhere their name is attached; and the moment the setting turns inward, can become someone you would not recognize.

40
00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:42.520
We have a great deal of material for agreeing with Glaucon.

41
00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:47.290
But right here there is a small and strange piece of evidence pointing the other way.

42
00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:54.460
It comes from an experiment carried out in two thousand six in the north of England, in the staff kitchen of a university department.

43
00:03:54.460 --> 00:04:01.470
In the kitchen there is an honesty box for tea and coffee; whoever takes some drops in the money, and nobody supervises it.

44
00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:10.240
The researchers hung an image above the price list that changed every week: some weeks a picture of flowers, some weeks a photograph of human eyes.

45
00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:17.890
In the weeks when the eyes were hanging there, the money dropped into the box rose to roughly three times what it was in the flower weeks.

46
00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:21.060
Nobody was watching anybody.

47
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.030
There was a piece of paper on the wall, that is all.

48
00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:30.760
Which means that a human being reacts not to a genuine observer but to the feeling of being observed.

49
00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:34.730
Something inside us is extraordinarily sensitive to a gaze.

50
00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:39.180
And at that empty intersection, a great many people still stop.

51
00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:49.230
There are people who run after a man to return the wallet they found when no one was looking, who carry the extra change back to the till, who keep a promise nobody will ever hear about.

52
00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:52.400
What stops them is not punishment.

53
00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.250
Then what is it?

54
00:04:55.250 --> 00:05:04.180
This is the question we will be pursuing in this episode: there is a voice inside, and sometimes that voice says the exact opposite of what serves us.

55
00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:06.550
Who put it there?

56
00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:13.880
In Western thought, the first great figure to listen to this voice and take it seriously was the man sitting opposite Glaucon.

57
00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:18.810
In his defence, Socrates speaks of a sign that had followed him since childhood.

58
00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:22.780
He calls it his daimonion; a divine indication.

59
00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:31.710
This indication has a very strange feature, and it is the feature that matters most in the history of philosophy: this voice never tells him what he should do.

60
00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:35.840
It only holds him back from something he is about to do.

61
00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.330
It does not command, it forbids.

62
00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:43.620
It does not point an arrow, it pulls him by the sleeve.

63
00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:50.790
Socrates offers this as the reason he stayed away from politics; but the truly striking scene takes place in court.

64
00:05:50.790 --> 00:06:01.160
In the year three hundred and ninety-nine before Christ, before a jury of more than five hundred citizens, he is accused of corrupting the young and of not recognising the gods of the city.

65
00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.370
He is sentenced to death.

66
00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:14.700
And on that day he says that the sign which had stopped him for years at the smallest wrong did not make a sound even once: not that morning as he left the house, not on the way to the court, not while he was speaking.

67
00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:19.630
For him, this is proof that what was happening was not a bad thing.

68
00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:25.360
So here is a man accepting his own death sentence by reading the silence of the voice inside him.

69
00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:30.770
The voice within is not a watchman of fear here; it is the very thing that quietens fear.

70
00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:40.020
Centuries later, Jean-Jacques Rousseau, in his book on education published in seventeen sixty-two, placed this voice in an almost divine position.

71
00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:47.990
In his view reason can deceive us; our interest makes reason say whatever we want, and cleverness finds a covering for everything.

72
00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.680
But conscience does not deceive.

73
00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:57.370
Rousseau described it as an unerring instinct planted inside the human being, an infallible judge of good and evil.

74
00:06:57.370 --> 00:07:05.820
This view was very beautiful, and also very fragile; because throughout history most of the people who did terrible things had an untroubled conscience.

75
00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:11.230
Immanuel Kant took the matter up from here and set it on far firmer ground.

76
00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:22.720
At the end of his Critique of Practical Reason, published in seventeen eighty-eight, he writes one of the most often repeated sentences in the history of philosophy: two things fill a person with ever new wonder,

77
00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.250
however long they are thought about.

78
00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:29.540
The starry sky above, and the moral law within.

79
00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:32.390
The pairing here is entirely deliberate.

80
00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:38.760
Kant treats the voice inside as something as real as the universe outside, as ordered as the laws of that universe.

81
00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:43.690
Just as the movement of the stars is not arbitrary, neither is morality.

82
00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:49.100
Kant's description of conscience, on the other hand, is cool and chilling.

83
00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:52.910
For him, conscience is a court set up inside the human being.

84
00:07:52.910 --> 00:08:00.400
And the oddity of this court is the following: you are the defendant, you are also the prosecutor, you are also the judge.

85
00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.210
What is more, you cannot withdraw from the case.

86
00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:14.260
A person can flee an outward court, leave the country, hire a lawyer, destroy the evidence; but there is no possibility of failing to appear at the hearing inside oneself.

87
00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:17.590
The session continues even while you sleep.

88
00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:23.960
The fine distinction Kant draws here should not be missed, because it is still constantly confused today.

89
00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:29.210
Conscience, in his account, is not the thing that tells you what is right.

90
00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:39.260
Finding out what is right is the work of reason; and for that Kant lays down his famous test: can you bear to turn the rule of the thing you are about to do into a law that everyone follows?

91
00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:44.990
If everyone lied the moment they gave their word, would there still be such a thing as giving your word?

92
00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:49.120
Making that calculation is not the work of conscience.

93
00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:53.890
The work of conscience is to judge whether you acted according to your own conviction.

94
00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:58.020
So conscience is not a compass but a witness.

95
00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:03.590
It does not know what you should do; it knows whether the thing you did agrees with what you yourself knew.

96
00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:06.440
We already live this in everyday life.

97
00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:13.770
On the day you keep a promise you told nobody about, nobody congratulates you; but inside there is a silent approval.

98
00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:24.460
And on the day you choose the easy thing, when you make that small calculation not a soul heard about, nobody reproaches you; and yet in the evening, in bed, after you have switched off the light,

99
00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:27.310
that court convenes of its own accord.

100
00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:33.040
This is exactly what Kant wanted to say: there is no way to close the courtroom.

101
00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:41.170
But at the end of the nineteenth century and the beginning of the twentieth, two figures appeared and put a far more uncomfortable question to this lovely picture.

102
00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:47.220
What if this voice is not a law that came down from the sky, but nothing more than a domesticated fear?

103
00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:55.990
In the work he published in nineteen twenty-three, Sigmund Freud added a third storey to the human mind: the superego.

104
00:09:55.990 --> 00:10:05.080
According to Freud, in the first years of life the child is surrounded by the prohibitions of the mother and father, by their looks, by the tone of their voice.

105
00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:11.450
In time these prohibitions do not stay outside; the child takes them in and makes them part of its own mind.

106
00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:18.620
And so a day comes when the mother and father are not in the room, are perhaps not even alive, but the voice is still there.

107
00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:25.950
Freud's claim was this: the thing we call conscience is, to a large extent, an authority that has moved in with us.

108
00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:31.040
The observer from outside has settled inside, and is now working without wages.

109
00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:36.610
There is one oddity Freud noticed about this, and it is what makes the matter genuinely dark.

110
00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:47.300
In the book he wrote on civilisation in nineteen thirty, he says that the harshest consciences often come not from the harshest upbringing but, on the contrary, from the most affectionate homes.

111
00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:49.030
Why?

112
00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:54.920
Because a child cannot direct anger at a mother and father it loves and who do it no harm.

113
00:10:54.920 --> 00:11:01.610
And when it cannot be directed, that anger does not evaporate; it changes course and turns back on itself.

114
00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:07.020
So a person begins to inflict upon themselves a punishment nobody imposed on them.

115
00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:17.390
In Freud's account, the sense of guilt comes before the offence; a person can feel guilty without having done anything, because the prosecutor inside does not wait for the deed,

116
00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:20.400
it counts even the intention as sufficient.

117
00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:29.650
Friedrich Nietzsche, in his book investigating the origins of morality published in eighteen eighty-seven, went further still and described the birth scene of this voice.

118
00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:38.740
In his account, for tens of thousands of years the human being was a creature that hunted, attacked, fled, and discharged its strength outward.

119
00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:44.630
Then one day walls were raised; settled life came, the city, law, peace.

120
00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:48.760
All those instincts that had flowed outward were shut in at a stroke.

121
00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:53.210
But energy does not vanish, it changes direction.

122
00:11:53.210 --> 00:12:00.700
In Nietzsche's phrase, the human being turned inward: he turned upon himself the wildness he could no longer release outside.

123
00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:06.590
And on that day a creature was born that makes itself suffer, judges itself, tears at itself.

124
00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:09.760
Nietzsche gives this the name bad conscience.

125
00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:18.210
For him it is at the same time the story of how the human soul acquired depth: the human being grew deep thanks to the whip turned upon himself.

126
00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:22.180
The subtlety we gained was the invoice for the price we paid.

127
00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:26.790
He also points to an interesting detail of language that supports this thought.

128
00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:30.760
In German, the word meaning guilt also means debt.

129
00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:42.250
Setting out from this, Nietzsche says that at the root of the feeling of guilt stands not a divine law but a much older and much more concrete relationship, the relationship between creditor and debtor.

130
00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:45.740
An unpaid debt is one day collected in pain.

131
00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:51.790
And the pang of conscience, in his view, is the continuation of that collection inside us.

132
00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:58.960
Both of these figures point to the same place: perhaps the owner of the voice inside is not you.

133
00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:07.250
Perhaps it is your father's voice, perhaps the voice of the herd, perhaps the voice of a habit thousands of years old that taught you to whip yourself.

134
00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:10.580
So how strong is this voice?

135
00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:17.110
In nineteen sixty-one in America, a psychologist named Stanley Milgram set out to measure exactly that.

136
00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:21.560
Volunteers were told they were taking part in an experiment about learning.

137
00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:28.250
They would deliver an electric shock of steadily increasing severity to a person in the next room for every wrong answer.

138
00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:41.020
The person in the next room was in fact part of the experiment and was receiving no shocks at all; but the volunteer, who did not know this, heard the protests from behind the wall, then the cries, and then the silence.

139
00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:47.390
Whenever the volunteer wanted to stop, the official in the white coat said in a calm voice: please continue.

140
00:13:47.390 --> 00:13:50.240
The responsibility is mine.

141
00:13:50.240 --> 00:14:00.290
In the first arrangement, twenty-six people out of forty, that is sixty-five percent, turned the dial all the way to the final level, up to four hundred and fifty volts.

142
00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:04.740
What this means is not that most people have no conscience.

143
00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:08.710
The truly frightening detail in the records is a different one.

144
00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:12.040
Almost everyone who continued was suffering.

145
00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:19.530
They were sweating, their hands were shaking, they were laughing strangely, they said over and over that they wanted to stop.

146
00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:22.860
Which means the voice inside was speaking.

147
00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:24.590
It was shouting.

148
00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:27.280
But it could not stop the hand.

149
00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:33.170
One sentence, a single sentence, was enough: the responsibility is mine.

150
00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:37.620
And this should not be forgotten either: thirty-five percent stopped.

151
00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:43.510
In the same room, facing the same white coat, hearing the same sentence, they stopped.

152
00:14:43.510 --> 00:14:49.560
So the voice is neither as solid as a settled law nor something that can be silenced altogether.

153
00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:57.690
It stands somewhere; sometimes it is pressed down, sometimes it rises to its feet.

154
00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:01.500
And when it is silenced, where does it go?

155
00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:07.710
While following the trail of that question, the word we use in Turkish gives us a clue of its own.

156
00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:13.440
Vicdan, the Turkish word for conscience, comes from an Arabic root meaning to find.

157
00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:19.970
From the same root comes vecd, the state of finding, the state of being carried out of oneself.

158
00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:27.780
Which is to say that conscience, in the memory of the word itself, is not a rule learned from outside but something found inside.

159
00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:31.110
Not a piece of knowledge, but a finding.

160
00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:38.600
Islamic thought approaches this matter not with a single voice inside the human being but with layers that contend with one another.

161
00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:43.530
In the Quran, three states of the nafs, the self, are mentioned.

162
00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:46.540
One is the self that commands evil.

163
00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:51.470
One is the self that has come to peace, that has made peace with itself.

164
00:15:51.470 --> 00:15:57.360
And between the two stands the self that reproaches itself; its name is nafs-i lawwama.

165
00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:07.410
At the opening of the chapter of the Resurrection something remarkable happens: the oath is sworn not by the self that has come to peace, but by the self that reproaches itself.

166
00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:12.500
Which means that what is honoured here is not inner peace; it is restlessness itself.

167
00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:22.550
A person turning back on themselves after what they have done and calling themselves to account is treated in this tradition not as a weakness but as the precious side of being human.

168
00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:27.480
A person who does not reproach themselves has not been promoted, but blunted.

169
00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:36.250
A conversation attributed to the Prophet Muhammad and recorded in the collections of hadith carries the matter somewhere that is still surprising today.

170
00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:40.380
A man named Wabisa comes to ask what righteousness and sin are.

171
00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:43.390
The answer he receives is not a list.

172
00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:47.680
He is told to put the very question he came to ask to his own heart.

173
00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:56.610
Righteousness is what the heart settles into, what the self finds peace in; sin is what circles inside you like a wound, what scratches at the soul.

174
00:16:56.610 --> 00:17:05.060
Another saying, among the sound reports collected by Muslim, gives the same measure: sin is what you would not want other people to find out about.

175
00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:11.750
Notice what this means: the measure is not the chance of being caught; it is shame itself.

176
00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:19.400
Even if you had put on the ring of Gyges, that shame would stay exactly where it is, because there is already someone who sees you.

177
00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:24.810
But the most realistic side of this tradition is that it does not claim this voice is flawless.

178
00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:28.620
Ghazali likens the human heart to a mirror.

179
00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:32.590
When the mirror is clean it shows the truth in front of it as it is.

180
00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:36.880
But every wrong leaves a stain on it, a layer of rust.

181
00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:45.330
When the stains pile one on another the mirror is not damaged, not broken, and does not move from its place; it simply stops showing.

182
00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:49.460
This is precisely what the silencing of conscience is.

183
00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:53.590
That voice does not die, it becomes inaudible.

184
00:17:53.590 --> 00:18:00.760
And the most dangerous thing that can happen to a person is not the loss of the voice but the failure to notice the day the voice stopped.

185
00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:06.330
Because as long as you are troubled you are still there; when the trouble ends, everything looks fine.

186
00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:12.860
In our own tradition of thought, the one who perhaps said this inner presence most plainly is Yunus Emre.

187
00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:17.150
There is an I within me, deeper in than me, he says.

188
00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:20.160
The whole matter in a single line.

189
00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:26.050
Someone further inside than the person themselves, belonging to them yet not taking their orders.

190
00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:29.860
You cannot lie to them, because they are in the same room as you.

191
00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:34.950
You cannot talk them round, because they have heard all your justifications too.

192
00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:41.960
In more recent times, the figure who placed this voice at the centre of a moral philosophy was Nurettin Topçu.

193
00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:49.770
In the doctoral work he defended in Paris in nineteen thirty-four, he examined the tension between conforming and rebelling.

194
00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:54.540
Where he arrived was this: morality is not doing what the crowd does.

195
00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:59.310
Doing what everyone does is not being moral, it is only avoiding notice.

196
00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:03.920
Morality begins where a person's conscience is able to object to the crowd.

197
00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:13.010
For Topçu, real rebellion is not a noisy quarrel but a person's ability to stay faithful to the voice inside them while everyone else is saying the opposite.

198
00:19:13.010 --> 00:19:18.420
And today, nobody needs to send us an official in a white coat to silence that voice.

199
00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:23.510
We silence it ourselves, and with entirely reasonable sentences at that.

200
00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:25.880
Everyone does it.

201
00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:28.410
My share in it is tiny.

202
00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:30.940
That is how the system works.

203
00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:34.110
If I don't do it, somebody else will.

204
00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:40.800
Every one of these sentences may be true; not one of them refutes that voice, they only turn down its volume.

205
00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:49.090
What that single sentence did in Milgram's experiment, we do to ourselves every day: we hand the responsibility over to somewhere else.

206
00:19:49.090 --> 00:19:54.660
To the question of who put the voice inside you, these thinkers do not give the same answer.

207
00:19:54.660 --> 00:20:06.470
For some, God put it there; for some, it is the very structure of reason; for some, a voice you heard in the first years of childhood; for some, a whip that has been turned inward for thousands of years.

208
00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:10.600
This argument is not finished, and probably never will be.

209
00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:19.530
But there is one point on which they all quietly agree, and that is the point that really matters: this voice does not fall silent even when it has lost the argument.

210
00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:27.500
Even on the nights when you have proved yourself right, won the debate, and nobody could say a word against you, something goes on remaining inside.

211
00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:34.670
Perhaps that is the only proof that a conscience is alive: the debt you still feel is unpaid after you have closed every account.

212
00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:41.680
At three in the morning, waiting at the empty intersection in front of the red light, you may not know who it is you are waiting for.

213
00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:46.290
But if you are waiting, it means there is someone there besides you.

214
00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:50.100
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

215
00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:53.750
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
